#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""crewboss: install and run your own Crewboss (app + Postgres in Docker, bound to 127.0.0.1). Stdlib only, Python 3.10+.

    ./crewboss install            ask a few questions (or pass flags + --yes), then build, start and make the owner account
    ./crewboss up | down | restart | status | logs [-f] [service]
    ./crewboss owner [--reset]    create (or reset) the owner account of a running install; prints a one-time temporary password
    ./crewboss deploy-test        a throwaway fresh install of the exact snapshot, checked and deleted (docs/deploy-test.md)
    ./crewboss reset-owner        the owner lost their passkey AND recovery codes: print a new temporary password (server shell only)

Where things live (task #707, change #62). Everything of one install is in ONE folder, the instance folder (default
~/.crewboss, `--home DIR`):

    instance.yaml    this install's settings (name, public_url, sign-in, certificate check ...)
    compose.env      the env file the CLI hands to docker compose (project name, port, database password, folders). 0600.
                     It is NOT the repo's own `.env`, which is never read or written here.
    secrets/         0700: ssh-ca-enc-key, vapid-private.pem, runner-identity-key (0600). Back them up with the database.
    materials/       the shared materials folder
    compose.override.yml  optional: this install's extra mounts / add-ons, added as a second -f when present
    Caddyfile        only with the bundled proxy
    proxy/           only with "I already run a reverse proxy": ready-made Caddy and nginx snippets

Every docker compose call is `docker compose -p <project> --env-file <home>/compose.env --project-directory <checkout>
-f <checkout>/docker-compose.yml [-f <home>/compose.override.yml] [--profile caddy] ...` with every COMPOSE_*, CREWBOSS_* and POSTGRES_* variable removed from the
environment, so it can never act on another project (the project name always comes from compose.env).

Certificates: a network install always uses a publicly trusted certificate (Let's Encrypt). Plain http is for localhost only.
Either you already run a reverse proxy with HTTPS (we print a snippet), or the bundled Caddy gets the certificate by HTTP-01
(ports 80 and 443 must reach this machine). The app itself refuses to start without a good certificate (`tls_check: enforce`).
"""
